#~ "an empty row follows every return "
#~ "and jump, conditional ones too, to "
#~ "set the blocks of code apart. "
#~ "**Block separator** in the **Options** "
#~ "menu switches these rows off."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "ROM is protected: changes to a ROM"
#~ " address are not written unless "
#~ "**Allow ROM wr** in the **Options** "
#~ "menu is ticked. A cartridge is "
#~ "never written."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "The Labels menu of the toolbar has:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**Labels list** in the Labels menu, "
#~ "or in the right-click menu of "
#~ "the listing, opens it."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**Group** - the set of labels in"
#~ " effect. The buttons beside it rename"
#~ " the set, add a new empty one"
#~ " and delete it."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**View** changes every byte of the "
#~ "block, when the cursor row is "
#~ "inside it;"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**Fill...** in the Tools menu starts "
#~ "with the block's addresses (see [Memory"
#~ "](debugger-memory.md))."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "Each byte of memory can be marked"
#~ " as code or as one kind of "
#~ "data, and the listing shows it "
#~ "that way. Select a row or a "
#~ "block, then pick from **View** in "
#~ "the toolbar or the right-click "
#~ "menu:"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**Runtime mapping** in the **Options** "
#~ "menu marks memory as the program "
#~ "runs: every byte the CPU runs as"
#~ " an instruction becomes **Opcode**, and "
#~ "every byte it reads or writes as"
#~ " data and that has no mark yet"
#~ " becomes **Byte**. It works while the"
#~ " machine runs, after the Debugger is"
#~ " closed. Play the program for a "
#~ "while, open the Debugger again, and "
#~ "the data shows as data. **Clear "
#~ "mapping** takes every mark away, yours"
#~ " too, after asking."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "**Breakpoints**"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "**Labels**"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "**View**"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "**Trace**"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "**Tools**"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "**Options**"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "(circular arrows)"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid "(floppy disk)"
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**Disasm to file** in the floppy "
#~ "disk menu writes the listing to a"
#~ " text file you name: the selected "
#~ "block, or all 64K with nothing "
#~ "selected. The file starts with an "
#~ "`ORG` line; labels are written as "
#~ "`name:`, comments as they are, "
#~ "instructions after a tab. It can "
#~ "go back into an assembler."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "**Save map** and **Load map** in "
#~ "the floppy disk menu write and "
#~ "read it. Loading a map replaces "
#~ "all label sets and comments. Comments"
#~ " are only kept in a memory map,"
#~ " never in a labels file, so "
#~ "save a map when you have written"
#~ " comments. A map does not hold "
#~ "breakpoints: they have a file of "
#~ "their own (see [Breakpoints and watching"
#~ "](debugger-breakpoints.md))."
#~ msgstr ""

#~ msgid ""
#~ "The button with the circular arrows "
#~ "opens the last file again, the way"
#~ " it was opened (see [Opening a "
#~ "file](files.md)), and reads the labels "
#~ "file you loaded again. Then the "
#~ "listing goes to PC. Use it after"
#~ " building a new version of your "
#~ "program: the new code and its new"
#~ " labels come in with one click. "
#~ "The **Reload file** key does the "
#~ "same; it has no key out of "
#~ "the box."
#~ msgstr ""

